artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a finite harmonic-oscillator shell-model setting in which neutron and proton one-body densities $\rho_n$ and $\rho_p$ are expanded in a finite basis and represented by finite vectors of density-polynomial coefficients $(C_{n,1},\dots,C_{n,N_n})$ and $(C_{p,1},\dots,C_{p,N_p})$. For a given many-body Hamiltonian, write the energy of a configuration-mixing trial state as a polynomial in the mixing coefficients, and impose the Levy–Lieb density constraints (which fix the densities $\rho_n$ and $\rho_p$ in terms of the mixing coefficients) together with the normalization constraint as algebraic polynomial equations in those mixing coefficients. After eliminating the constrained mixing variables and the surviving coefficients via polynomial elimination, one obtains an implicit polynomial relation
$$R(\eta, C_{n,1},\dots,C_{n,N_n}, C_{p,1},\dots,C_{p,N_p}) \;=\; 0$$
that links the minimal energy $\eta$ (as a function of the density) to the density parameters, thereby defining an implicit polynomial density functional.

Develop and justify rigorous algebraic strategies that:
(i) practically handle the very large multivariate polynomials $R$ produced by elimination when the number of parameters is large;
(ii) systematically compare two such implicit relations $R$ and $R'$ obtained from two different finite-basis choices, and quantify in what sense they define the same (or different) density functional;
(iii) provide controlled extrapolation procedures for $\eta$ as a function of the density parameters when direct elimination becomes computationally infeasible, with rigorous error or convergence guarantees;
(iv) embed the resulting rigorous polynomial density functionals into larger nuclear many-body models in a usable way.

